rite the detailed review\nto `/audit-output/REVIEW.md` and preserve reviewer-authored scripts plus\nbounded command logs below `/audit-output/evidence/`. Record exact commands,\nexit statuses, and relevant output. Do not write anywhere else outside\n`/tmp/audit-work` and `/audit-output`.\n\nTrusted inputs are `/reference/canonical.py`, `/reference/prompt.py`, and\n`/reference/py2mpy.py`. Apply this condition-aware semantics boundary before\nauditing:\n\n- In `SUPPLIED_SEMANTICS` mode,\n  `/reference/reference-semantics` must be present. Recursively compare the\n  candidate's `reference-semantics/` with that trusted tree. Missing, additional,\n  changed, mistyped, or symlinked candidate entries are integrity failures. The\n  trusted baseline does not bless proof-specific rules in `verification.k`.\n- In `GENERATED_SEMANTICS` mode,\n  `/reference/reference-semantics` must not exist. Do not search for, request,\n  infer, or use a hidden reference semantics. Audit the candidate's own\n  `semantic.k` and helper K files on their merits. Minimal coverage is acceptable\n  when it soundly covers every construct the submitted program actually uses;\n  missing semantics for unused constructs is not a defect.\n\nIf the trusted mounts contradict the rendered semantics mode, document the\ninfrastructure breach in `REVIEW.md`, omit candidate verdict markers, and stop;\nan infrastructure failure must become `AUDIT_ERROR`, never `FAIL`.\n\n## Required seven-stage audit\n\nPerform all seven stages in order. The audit requires both a fresh dynamic\nreconstruction and an exhaustive static review; neither gate can substitute for\nthe other.\n\n### 1. Input and provenance integrity\n\nFirst read the launcher-owned `/audit-input.json`, including its\n`record_layout`, `container_paths`, recorded hashes, and integrity fields.\nIndependently hash and inspect the mounted inputs rather than following the\nhost-only provenance paths stored in that file.\n\nRead `/audit-campaign-lock.json` and r